\pard\plain \tx747\tx1520\tx2240\tx3013\sl264\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 This project contains the text of both the Mac and Windows editions of the PDF. As the large majority of the text is the same between the two, this is achieved through a layered approach of marking text as pertaining to one or the other platform exclusively. These techniques may be of interest to anyone that needs to produce multiple editions from one common draft folder:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\pard\plain \tx270\tx540\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\li270\fi-270\sb60\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 <$Scr_Ps::0>1. A custom metadata  field, "Platform" is used to toggle an entire section between macOS Only, Windows Only, or All. For your convenience, this field has been added to the Outliner column set, but you can also keep the Inspector opened to the Metadata tab, and consult the setting in the middle "Custom Metadata" section.}
\par\pard\plain \pardeftab580\tx270\tx540\tx1680\tx2240\tx2800\tx3360\tx3920\tx4480\tx5040\tx5600\tx6160\tx6720\li270\fi-270\sb60\ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \tab When compiling, this metadata field is used to filter out the opposing platform-only sections. This project has been set up to compile the Windows version of the manual, and if you click the funnel icon in the Contents compile tab, you'll find that it is set to filter out "macOS Only" items.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 2. At the text level, styles are used to mark text as being Mac or Windows-only. You will note spots of text that look like this. }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 <$Scr_Cs::1>What you are reading now would only appear in the macOS edition.<!$Scr_Cs::1>}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 <$Scr_Cs::2> Now you are reading text that would only appear in the Windows edition.<!$Scr_Cs::2>}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  If you examine the project's stylesheet, you'll find there are in fact a number of this, thanks to how there are other functional statements that may need to be made within such a range.}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 <$Scr_Cs::2> This text for Windows includes a keyboard shortcut (<!$Scr_Cs::2>}{\f1\fs20\b1\i0\cf4 <$Scr_Cs::3>Alt+F4<!$Scr_Cs::3>}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 <$Scr_Cs::2>).<!$Scr_Cs::2><!$Scr_Ps::0>}
\par\pard\plain \tx747\tx1520\tx2240\tx3013\sl264\slmult1\ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 It might be a little awkward at first, but like me, you'll probably soon }{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "scrivcmt://4AF65232-BDA1-47CF-8EF5-617135E52BCE"}{\fldrslt\f0\fs24\b0\i0 start reading only the text you want to read}}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 , and habitually check the inspector Platform setting when going through sections.}}